{
  "obs_data_name": "wanaverbecq2003_scg",
  "reference_key": "wanaverbecq2003plasma",
  "calibration_note": "wanaverbecq2003plasma (rat SCG, indo-1, 33 degC): resting [Ca2+]i 102 +- 4 nM and mono-exponential recovery of small Ca loads, tau ~5 s after a 100 nM rise and ~2.5 s after a 500 nM rise (Fig. 1C), PMCA the only extruder at these loads (Na-free and thapsigargin without effect); fitted in the soma Ca handling (SN_soma_Argus2026_v01, its own buffer, binding ratio ~100 against Wanaverbecq's ~500) with SERCA and the ER leak off, rho_PMCA and a tonic Ca entry (Ca_influx leak, not applied) fitted. Result: j_max = A_mem rho_PMCA r_PMCA / N_A = 385 amol/s on the 3,000 um^2 soma (r_PMCA 30 /s), within the 42-470 amol/s bracket of colegrove2000quantitative and wanaverbecq2003plasma; the model recovers with tau ~3.5 s after both loads (a compromise: the shell-bulk exchange limits it to >= ~2.4 s)",
  "comment": "Wanaverbecq, Marsh, Al-Qatari & Brown 2003 (J Physiol 550:83), cultured rat superior cervical ganglion neurons, perforated patch, indo-1, 33 degC. Data: resting [Ca2+]i 102 +- 4 nM (mean +- SEM, n = 83; Results, first section), maintained against a tonic La3+-sensitive Ca leak; after Ca loads up to 500 nM the recovery is mono-exponential with tau ~5 s at a 100 nM rise, shortening to ~2.5 s at a 500 nM rise (Fig. 1C, tau against the amplitude); the PMCA (pH 9 or carboxyeosin) sets both; Na-free solution (no NCX) and thapsigargin (no SERCA) leave small transients unchanged. The values are those read from the full text (job notes tau_provenance_rest.md, section 19); the figure could not be screenshotted (no PDF on disk). Set-up (validation.wanaverbecq2003_scg.harness): the PMCA (mod, this instance) on the soma Ca handling Ca_handling SN_soma_Argus2026_v01 (default instance: shell 0.1 um under 3,000 um^2, bulk of 7,500 um^3, fixed buffer 8 mM with Kd 80 uM, i.e. binding ratio kappa ~100 near rest; Wanaverbecq's kappa is ~500 at 100 nM, so this calibration gives the pump rate that reproduces the measured recovery WITH THE MODEL'S BUFFER; with kappa 500 the same tau would need ~5 x j_max), with SERCA and the pre-ER leak set to zero (rho_SERCA_Ca, rho_leak_Ca_Ca = 0: thapsigargin had no effect, so the data are the PMCA alone; the ER is then isolated) and no NCX (Na-free had no effect). Two Ca_influx records: 'leak', the tonic Ca entry that holds rest (Wanaverbecq's La3+-sensitive leak; fitted as a nuisance parameter, j_Ca_influx, so that the rest datum fixes it, and not applied to the model) and 'load', the brief Ca load (a square influx of 0.2 s, the depolarisation-evoked entry of the experiment), sized so that the model's peak rise is ~100 and ~500 nM at the calibrated pump. Observable: Ca_bulk, the bulk cytosol (96 % of the cytosolic volume; indo-1 reports the cell average). Protocol: pre_time 300 s at rest (the buffer and the leak-pump balance settle), sub-experiment 0 holds 10 s (resting Ca, the last 10 %), 1 loads 730 amol/s for 0.2 s, 2 records the recovery for 25 s, 3 loads 2360 amol/s for 0.2 s, 4 records the recovery for 15 s. Each recovery series is rest + delta exp(-t/tau) from the end of the load (102 nM + 100 nM e^(-t/5 s), 102 nM + 500 nM e^(-t/2.5 s)), std 10 % of delta; the first 0.5 s (the bulk still filling from the shell) have std 1 mM (not fitted). Temperature: Wanaverbecq 33 degC, the model 22 degC (the Hill pump has no temperature dependence here). To redo: edit this file and run venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_modules.py --component PMCA/Colegrove2000_v01 -k 'calibrate and wanaverbecq2003_scg' --include-unreviewed, then venv/bin/python -m cam_testing.calibrate apply PMCA/Colegrove2000_v01 wanaverbecq2003_scg",
